{"id":"7033e129-d329-4e91-8b38-c08691a24afb","object":"chat.completion","created":1784992197,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children_map.setdefault(n['parent'], []).append(n['id'])\\n    # Identify candidates: removable, not root, symbol not required\\n    candidates = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if (n['removable'] and n['id'] != root_id and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols):\\n            candidates.append(n)\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    # Initially all nodes present\\n    present_set = set(node_map.keys())\\n    for n in candidates:\\n        if n['id'] in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any child is still present (retained)\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        child_ids = children_map.get(n['id'], [])\\n        for cid in child_ids:\\n            if cid not in removed_set:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Check that all required symbols remain after removing this node\\n        # We simulate removal for the check\\n        # (since required symbols nodes are never removed, this pass will always succeed)\\n        # But do it formally:\\n        symbol_count = {}\\n        for nid in present_set:\\n            if nid != n['id']:\\n                sym = node_map[nid]['symbol']\\n                symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        all_required_remain = all(symbol_count.get(s, 0) > 0 for s in required_symbols)\\n        if not all_required_remain:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove\\n        removed_set.add(n['id'])\\n        removed_order.append(n['id'])\\n        present_set.discard(n['id'])\\n    # Retained are all nodes not removed\\n    retained_set = set(node_map.keys()) - removed_set\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained_set)\\n    # Ensure root and required symbols are included (they should be)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We are asked to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" on a parse tree. The input case has nodes, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. We need to determine which nodes to remove and which to retain, following rules.\n\nKey rules from description:\n- Do not remove root (parent null? root id? root is id \"root\"? But \"root\" is just a node id; but root node has parent null. So never remove the node with parent null? Actually \"never remove the root\" means the node that has no parent, i.e., parent == null. Also required_symbols: nodes with symbols that are in required_symbols must not be removed? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So:\n  - Root: node where parent is null.\n  - Required symbol: a node that has a symbol that is in required_symbols list. But careful: multiple nodes can have same symbol. Requirement says \"a required symbol\" – likely meaning any node whose symbol is one of the required symbols? But also \"a node with retained children\" - if we decide to retain a child, we must not remove its parent? Possibly: we can only remove a node if all its children are removed; otherwise we need to keep it to maintain tree structure? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that we decide to retain, we cannot remove that node. So removals must be bottom-up: we can only remove a node if all its descendants are also removed? Or at least if it has any child that we retain, we cannot remove it. So we must process in order of depth from deepest to shallowest (as described: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\")\n\n- Additionally: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, we must check that after removal, the set of symbols present in remaining nodes still includes all required_symbols. That is, removing that node must not cause any required symbol to disappear from the tree (if that node was the only one providing that symbol). So we need to consider the current set of nodes after tentative removals.\n\n- Use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth. So when processing nodes of same depth, order by ID (maybe alphabetical? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. We'll sort ascending string order.)\n\n- Return removed_node_ids (list of IDs in the order they were removed) and retained_node_ids (list of IDs of nodes that are not removed, sorted? The output schema says \"retained_node_ids\" is array of strings, \"removed_node_ids\" array of strings. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably ascending), and removed_node_ids in the order we removed them.\n\n- Use node depth as primary ordering: deeper first. For same depth, use node ID (string) as tie-breaker.\n\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This check should be done on the current state: after some removals already, we need to see if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be absent from the set of remaining nodes. If it would, then we cannot remove it, so we retain it.\n\n- Also: a node is removable only if its \"removable\" field is true. So only consider removable nodes for removal.\n\n- Also: we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. But children being retained is determined after processing deeper nodes first. So we need to keep track of which nodes are retained.\n\n- Implementation plan:\n  1. Build a mapping from node id to node object (dictionary).\n  2. Build a tree structure: for each node, list children ids.\n  3. Determine root node (node with parent null). We'll never remove it.\n  4. Collect all nodes that are removable (removable=true) and are not root and not required symbol (meaning symbol not in required_symbols). Actually the rule says never remove a required symbol, so any node whose symbol is in required_symbols must be retained. So we filter out nodes that are required symbols.\n  5. Sort these candidate nodes by depth descending, and for same depth by node id ascending.\n  6. Initialize set of removed_ids = set() to track removed nodes.\n  7. Initialize set of retained_ids = set() to track nodes we decide to retain (including root and required symbols initially). Actually we need a set of nodes that are currently present (not removed). Initially all nodes are present.\n  8. For each candidate node in sorted order:\n       a. Check if node is already removed? skip.\n       b. Check if any of its children are currently retained? That is, if any child id is in retained_set (which is the set of nodes that are not removed). But if a child was removed, then it's fine. But we must ensure: a node cannot be removed if it has any child that we have decided to retain (i.e., not removed). Since we process deeper nodes first, children will have been processed already. So we can check if child is in retained_set? Actually children that are not removed are retained. So we can check if there exists a child that is not removed (i.e., child id not in removed_set). That's equivalent: any child that is still present. Because if we haven't removed a child, we must retain it. So we can simply: if any child is not removed (not in removed_set), then we cannot remove this node.\n       c. Check if after removing this node, all required symbols are still represented. This means: for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (among nodes not removed, excluding this node) that has that symbol. So we need to compute the current symbol coverage from nodes not removed. We could maintain a count of nodes per symbol. Or just compute from scratch each time for simplicity, since number of nodes is small (7 in fixtures). But to be efficient, we can maintain a symbol count dictionary. Initially count each symbol from all nodes. When we remove a node, we decrement count for its symbol. When we tentatively remove a node, we need to check if after decrement, any required symbol count becomes 0. So we can simulate: if node's symbol is in required_symbols, then we cannot remove because required symbols must be retained? Actually the rule says \"never remove ... a required symbol\". So nodes with required symbols are excluded from candidate set. So we don't need to check that here. For other symbols, we need to ensure that the required symbols are still present after removal. So we maintain symbol counts. When we process a candidate node, we check if its symbol is required - but it's not because we filtered. So we just ensure that after removal, the count for each required symbol remains > 0. Since we filter required symbols out, the only way a required symbol could disappear is if the node being removed is the only node with that symbol? But that node doesn't have a required symbol. So actually the check might be more subtle: if a node has a symbol that is not required, its removal could impact required symbols? No, removing a node with a non-required symbol does not affect the count of required symbols. So we actually don't need to check? Wait, the description says: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" But if we never remove nodes with required symbols, then removing any other node doesn't change the presence of required symbols. So why the check? Perhaps there could be a scenario where a required symbol appears only on a node that is not removable? Yes, but we are not removing it. So the condition is automatically satisfied. However, consider that a node might be \"required symbol\" but not removable? Actually removable is a separate field. A node could be marked \"removable: true\" even if its symbol is required? The rule says never remove a required symbol, so we should also exclude nodes that are required symbol even if removable. So we filter them out. So after that, any removal won't affect required symbols. So maybe the condition is redundant in this simple case. But to be safe, we can still check: only proceed if for each req_sym in required_symbols, there is at least one node (among not removed, excluding this node) that has that symbol. But as argued, it's always true because we aren't removing those. So we can skip, but include for correctness.\n\n  9. If both conditions satisfied (no retained children, required symbols remain), then we mark this node as removed: add to removed_set, and record in removed_order list. Also update symbol counts? Actually we don't need to track symbol counts for non-required symbols. But we might need for the condition: we already satisfied.\n\n  10. After processing all candidate nodes, the retained nodes are all nodes not in removed_set. Also root and required symbols are automatically retained because they are not in candidate set? Wait: what if a required symbol node is also removable? We excluded it. So it's retained. So we can get retained_ids as all node ids minus removed_set. Then sort them (ascending) for output.\n\n  11. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained_ids)}.\n\nEdge cases: The root might have id not \"root\"? But given fixtures, it's \"root\". But we should identify root as node with parent null.\n\nAlso: \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has any child that we decide to retain, we cannot remove it. Since we process deeper first, children that we retain will be those that we did not remove (because they were not removable or failed conditions). So we can check children's status.\n\nImplementation details: We'll parse case payload. Use standard library only.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) that takes a dict. The case has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n\nWe'll return dict as per schema.\n\nLet's code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":2759,"total_tokens":4928,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2148},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}